import json,random
from pathlib import Path
questions=[]
def q(topic,stem,answer,others,explanation,first,last=None,kind='slides'):
 options=[answer]+others.split('|')
 assert 4<=len(options)<=10,(stem,len(options))
 assert len(set(options))==len(options),stem
 random.Random(len(questions)+1914).shuffle(options)
 questions.append(dict(id=f'q{len(questions)+1:03}',topic=topic,stem=stem,options=options,answer=options.index(answer),explanation=explanation,source={'kind':kind,'first':first,'last':last or first}))
q('Causes','Which distinction best explains the relationship between MAIN and the assassination in Sarajevo?','MAIN describes long-term pressures; the assassination triggered a particular crisis.','MAIN describes the decisions taken after the assassination; the assassination created the rivalries.|MAIN identifies wartime strategies; the assassination explains the failure of those strategies.|MAIN describes the peace settlement; the assassination explains the territorial clauses.', 'Militarism, alliances, imperialism, and nationalism existed before June 1914. The assassination activated those tensions in a specific diplomatic crisis.',3,4)
q('Causes','A government argues that peace depends on a permanently battle-ready army and expanding its navy. Which concept best fits?','Militarism','Nationalism|Imperialism|Self-determination|Collective security','The defining features are the glorification of military strength and continual readiness for war. National pride or colonial ambitions may accompany it, but they are not what the statement directly describes.',6)
q('Causes','How could an arms race make countries less secure even while they increased their forces?','Rivals could interpret defensive preparations as threats and accelerate their own armament.','Larger armies automatically ended the need for military alliances.|Increased spending forced all governments to follow identical foreign policies.|New weapons made governments unable to mobilize their civilian industries.|Naval competition removed the possibility of economic rivalry.|Military growth guaranteed that political disputes would be settled by arbitration.','Each state’s search for security could alarm its neighbors and intensify competition. More weapons did not necessarily produce greater mutual trust.',6,7)
q('Causes','Which rivalry is the clearest naval-arms-race example in the slides?','Britain and Germany','France and Russia|Italy and Austria-Hungary|Serbia and the Ottoman Empire','Britain was concerned about the growth of Germany’s navy. The other pairs relate to alliances or regional rivalries rather than this specific naval competition.',7)
q('Causes','Why did German unification worsen relations with France?','France lost Alsace-Lorraine, while Germany feared French efforts to reverse the defeat.','France gained Alsace-Lorraine, while Germany sought compensation through colonies.|France and Germany merged their armies, creating disagreements over command.|France’s alliance with Britain required Germany to restore the Ottoman Empire.|Germany’s unification dissolved Austria-Hungary and transferred its land to France.','The loss of Alsace-Lorraine linked French resentment to German fears of retaliation. Those fears encouraged further alliance-building.',9)
q('Causes','Which set correctly identifies the Triple Entente?','Britain, France, Russia','Germany, Austria-Hungary, Italy|Britain, France, Italy|France, Russia, Serbia|Britain, Russia, Germany|Germany, Austria-Hungary, Ottoman Empire','The prewar Triple Entente linked Britain, France, and Russia. Italy later fought with the Allies, but it was not one of the three Entente powers.',10,14)
q('Causes','Which state was in the prewar Triple Alliance but joined the Allies in 1915?','Italy','Germany|Austria-Hungary|Bulgaria|Ottoman Empire|Russia|Romania','Italy’s change in alignment is why the prewar Triple Alliance must be distinguished from the wartime Central Powers.',14)
q('Causes','What strategic danger did the Franco-Russian partnership pose for Germany?','A war against major enemies to both its west and east','The loss of all access to the Baltic Sea before fighting began|A requirement to defend French territory against Russia|An immediate naval blockade by two Mediterranean powers','France lay to Germany’s west and Russia to its east. Their alliance was central to German fears of a two-front war.',10,15)
q('Causes','What do the dominoes and linked threats in the alliance cartoons mainly illustrate?','A local dispute could spread as other powers chose to support their partners.','Each state had identical military goals and resources.|All treaties required immediate war regardless of circumstances.|Nationalist movements could operate without any great-power involvement.|Alliances prevented disputes involving nonmember states.','The images illustrate connected risks and escalation. They simplify the process, so avoid treating the cartoon as proof that leaders had no choices.',16,18)
q('Causes','Which statement best describes the relationship between nationalism and multiethnic empires?','Claims that cultural communities should govern themselves could threaten imperial unity.','Nationalism required every ethnic group to accept the borders of an existing empire.|Nationalism applied to overseas colonies but had little relevance within Europe.|Nationalism eliminated disagreements between people sharing a language.|Nationalism made cultural identity less important than membership in a dynasty.|Nationalism meant all political borders already matched ethnic boundaries.','Nationalist demands could split empires containing many peoples. The ethnic and political maps show why cultural and state boundaries did not neatly coincide.',21,22)
q('Causes','Why did Austria-Hungary fear Serbian ambitions in Bosnia-Herzegovina?','A South Slav nationalist movement could attract Slavic subjects away from Habsburg rule.','Serbia sought to return all Balkan territories to the Ottoman sultan.|Serbian expansion would directly transfer Alsace-Lorraine to France.|Serbia’s navy threatened to surpass the British fleet.','The threat was both territorial and internal: Serbian nationalism challenged control of Bosnia-Herzegovina and could inspire other Slavic populations.',25)
q('Causes','Which combination best explains the Balkans’ “powder keg” reputation?','Nationalist ambitions, imperial decline, territorial disputes, and great-power backing','Stable borders, shared political institutions, and limited outside involvement|A single unified Slavic government and the absence of imperial competition|Naval competition in the Atlantic and disputes over North American colonies|Rapid industrialization without competing national or territorial claims','The region combined local conflicts with the interests of Russia, Austria-Hungary, Germany, and the declining Ottoman Empire.',23,26)
q('Causes','Which term most precisely describes extending a state’s control over other peoples and territories?','Imperialism','Militarism|Nationalism|Industrialization|Mobilization|Self-determination|Rationing|Collective security','Imperialism concerns control beyond a state’s existing core territory. Militarism concerns military values and readiness; nationalism concerns national identity and political claims.',27,29)
q('Causes','How did industrialization affect the war described in the slides?','It supported mass production and supply while increasing destructive power.','It removed the need for civilian labor once armies were mobilized.|It made military technology less important than dynastic relationships.|It guaranteed rapid breakthroughs wherever factories produced weapons.|It prevented governments from sustaining long conflicts.|It replaced competition for resources with equal access to raw materials.','Industrial economies could equip large armies and sustain war. Increased firepower made attacks more costly rather than ensuring a short conflict.',30,31)
q('Causes','Why were Germany and Italy relatively late to the competition for colonies?','Their national unification occurred later than the development of older European empires.','They had controlled most overseas colonies until 1914 and then withdrew.|Their governments had already accepted a League ban on imperial expansion.|Their territories lacked any access to European sea routes.','The slides connect late entry to relatively late state-building; Japan’s emergence as a modern power followed a different modernization process.',29)
q('Causes','A poster says national honor requires supporting the country even at great personal cost. Which cause is it primarily drawing upon?','Nationalism','Imperialism|Industrialization|Alliance commitments|Military mobilization','The appeal makes loyalty to the nation a personal duty. It may encourage enlistment, but the persuasive mechanism is national identity and pride.',20,21)
q('1914 crisis','Which event was the immediate trigger of the 1914 crisis?','The assassination of Franz Ferdinand in Sarajevo','Austria-Hungary’s annexation of Bosnia-Herzegovina|German unification after the Franco-Prussian War|The signing of the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk|The expansion of the German navy|Italy’s entry into the Allied coalition','The assassination occurred on June 28, 1914. Annexation, unification, and naval rivalry were earlier developments; the other events came during the war.',33,34)
q('1914 crisis','What position did Franz Ferdinand hold when he was assassinated?','Heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne','Reigning emperor of Germany|Prime minister of Serbia|Tsar of Russia|Ottoman sultan|President of France|Commander of the British Expeditionary Force','Franz Ferdinand was the Habsburg heir, making the attack a direct challenge to Austria-Hungary’s ruling dynasty.',33,34)
q('1914 crisis','Which figure carried out the Sarajevo assassination?','Gavrilo Princip','Kaiser Wilhelm II|Tsar Nicholas II|Vittorio Orlando|Georges Clemenceau|David Lloyd George|Mustafa Kemal|Woodrow Wilson|T. E. Lawrence','Princip was a Bosnian Serb nationalist. The slide associates the assassination with the Black Hand; more precisely, the plot had support from figures linked to that network.',33,34)
q('1914 crisis','Which sequence correctly orders the opening declarations of war?','Austria-Hungary against Serbia → Germany against Russia → Germany against France → Britain against Germany','Germany against France → Austria-Hungary against Serbia → Britain against Germany → Germany against Russia|Britain against Germany → Germany against Russia → Austria-Hungary against Serbia → Germany against France|Austria-Hungary against Serbia → Britain against Germany → Germany against France → Germany against Russia','The key dates are July 28, August 1, August 3, and August 4, 1914. The sequence shows how the crisis expanded beyond the Balkans.',36)
q('1914 crisis','Which immediate development was central to Britain’s entry in August 1914?','Germany’s invasion of Belgium','The signing of the German-Russian armistice|Italy’s withdrawal from the Paris conference|The Zimmermann proposal to Mexico|The Bolshevik seizure of power','The German advance through Belgium brought the Western European strategy into direct conflict with British commitments and security concerns.',36,50)
q('1914 crisis','Who are “Nicky” and “Willy” in the slides?','Nicholas II of Russia and Wilhelm II of Germany','Nicholas II of Russia and Woodrow Wilson of the United States|Franz Ferdinand of Austria-Hungary and Wilhelm II of Germany|George V of Britain and Vittorio Orlando of Italy|Georges Clemenceau of France and David Lloyd George of Britain|Nicholas II of Russia and Franz Ferdinand of Austria-Hungary','The names refer to the two monarchs and their personal telegram exchanges during the crisis. Personal connections did not resolve their states’ conflicting policies.',38,40)
q('1914 crisis','Which answer best handles the claim that the assassination alone caused WWI?','It identifies the trigger but leaves out the conditions and decisions that widened the conflict.','It is sufficient because alliances and military planning began only after June 1914.|It is mistaken because the assassination occurred after every major power had declared war.|It is sufficient because the assassin directly commanded each European army.','A complete explanation links the immediate event to longer-term rivalries and specific choices about support, mobilization, and war.',37,45)
q('1914 crisis','What is military mobilization?','Preparing and moving a state’s forces and resources for war','Signing a treaty that formally ends a war|Transferring a colony to a League mandate|Allowing an ethnic community to choose its government|Borrowing money from citizens through bonds','Mobilization changes military readiness and can appear threatening to rivals. It is different from a peace treaty, self-determination, or one particular method of war finance.',36,42)
q('Warfare','What was the central strategic aim of the Schlieffen Plan?','Defeat France quickly before concentrating forces against Russia','Defeat Russia first while avoiding all fighting in western Europe|Secure Ottoman territory before intervening against France|Use submarines to force a peace before any land invasion|Maintain permanent neutrality in Belgium while fighting a naval war|Capture Britain before mobilizing against France','The plan sought to avoid a prolonged two-front war through a rapid western victory, including an advance through Belgium.',50,52)
q('Warfare','What was a major result of the failure to defeat France quickly in 1914?','Germany faced a prolonged two-front war and a Western Front stalemate.','Russia withdrew immediately and the Eastern Front disappeared.|The Allies accepted German control of Paris in return for an armistice.|Italy automatically entered the war alongside Germany.','The Allied counterattack helped stop the advance. Germany remained engaged in both east and west rather than winning the quick victory it had sought.',52,53)
q('Warfare','Which battle is associated with halting the German advance near Paris in 1914?','First Battle of the Marne','Battle of Verdun|Battle of the Somme|Gallipoli campaign|Battle of Tannenberg','The Marne belongs to 1914 and the failure of a rapid western victory. Verdun and the Somme are the major 1916 attritional battles emphasized later.',52,79)
q('Warfare','Which location best matches the Western Front in the slides?','Belgium and France','Russia and East Prussia|Serbia and the Ottoman Balkans|Iraq and Palestine|German East Africa and South Africa|Northern Italy and the Adriatic','The Western Front’s trench systems ran mainly through Belgium and France. Other areas belonged to different fronts or theaters.',79)
q('Warfare','What common lesson do Verdun and the Somme illustrate?','Industrial warfare could produce enormous losses for limited territorial gains.','A single naval victory could end a major land campaign.|New weapons always allowed attackers to bypass prepared defenses.|The Eastern Front had become entirely immobile by 1916.|Territorial gains were generally proportional to casualties.|Trenches had ceased to matter once tanks appeared.|Civilian production no longer influenced military endurance.','The slides compare very large casualties with advances of only a few miles. This is the logic of costly attrition rather than decisive maneuver.',54)
q('Warfare','In a battle report, what does “casualties” generally include?','Killed, wounded, missing, or captured personnel','Only soldiers confirmed killed on the battlefield|Only soldiers wounded who later recovered|All troops mobilized, including those never in combat','Casualties and deaths are not interchangeable. Keeping the categories separate is essential when interpreting the unit’s battle statistics.',54,112)
q('Warfare','Why did machine guns strengthen defenders?','Rapid fire could hit repeated waves of exposed attackers.','They eliminated the need for ammunition supplies.|They made barbed wire easier for attacking infantry to cross.|They prevented artillery from firing at fortified positions.|They allowed troops to travel underwater toward enemy lines.','Machine-gun fire was especially dangerous to soldiers crossing open ground. Its effect combined with wire, artillery, and defensive shelter.',55,60)
q('Warfare','Which weapon was an armored vehicle using tracks to cross difficult ground?','Tank','Submarine|Machine gun|Field artillery|Reconnaissance aircraft|Poison-gas canister|Torpedo|Armored battleship','Tracks and armor distinguish the tank. Britain first used tanks in battle at the Somme in 1916, but early models did not immediately end stalemate.',55)
q('Warfare','What role do aircraft play in the trench-system diagram?','Observing enemy troop buildups and warning of attacks','Moving entire infantry divisions across no man’s land|Replacing communication trenches as the main supply route|Preventing all use of long-range artillery','The diagram highlights reconnaissance. Observation could reveal preparations and help defenders respond before an assault.',60)
q('Warfare','What made barbed wire especially dangerous to attacking infantry?','It slowed movement while attackers remained exposed to defensive fire.','It absorbed artillery blasts and protected advancing troops.|It provided a concealed route between opposing trench systems.|It made it impossible for defenders to see approaching soldiers.|It replaced machine guns as the main long-range weapon.','Wire was an obstacle, not just a boundary. Its tactical value depended on delaying troops within range of guns.',60)
q('Warfare','Which description best fits no man’s land?','The exposed space between opposing front-line trench systems','A neutral country that refused to enter the war|A sheltered area behind a nation’s reserve trenches|Land administered by the League after the war|A naval zone closed only to military ships|Territory permanently declared free of artillery fire','No man’s land could be cratered, muddy, and covered by barbed wire and fire. It was dangerous contested ground, not legally neutral territory.',60)
q('Warfare','What was the main function of communication trenches?','Moving personnel and supplies between different trench lines','Serving as the open ground where opposing troops met|Providing the only positions from which aircraft could launch|Replacing reserve trenches with civilian housing','Communication trenches connected the system so movement did not require the same degree of exposure above ground.',60)
q('Warfare','Which statement about poison gas best fits the slides?','Both sides used it, and different gases caused severe injuries or death.','Only Germany ever used it, while the Allies relied entirely on gas masks.|It consistently made trench defenses unusable and ended stalemates.|It injured equipment rather than people and was used mainly against tanks.|It replaced artillery as the only weapon capable of reaching trenches.','Gas caused choking, burns, blindness, and other injuries; masks were a defensive response. It added suffering without guaranteeing a decisive breakthrough.',55)
q('Warfare','Which weapon most directly linked attacks at sea to civilian trade and supplies?','Submarine','Tank|Machine gun|Field artillery|Reconnaissance aircraft|Barbed wire|Gas mask|Trench mortar|Flamethrower','U-boats could sink merchant shipping with torpedoes, disrupting supplies and creating disputes involving neutral nations.',55,105)
q('Warfare','Why did new technology fail to make the war as short as many expected?','Industrial firepower often made defense stronger while both sides could sustain large armies.','New technology was available only to neutral states.|Factories stopped producing weapons once the war began.|Commanders abandoned mass armies in favor of very small forces.','The slides contrast “over by Christmas” with a long, costly war. Technology increased killing power, but that did not ensure successful offensives.',49,55)
q('Trench life','What primarily caused trench foot?','Prolonged exposure of the feet to wet and cold conditions','Inhalation of poison gas during an attack|Direct contact with barbed wire|A disease spread mainly by trench rats|A shock wave from an artillery shell','The testimony describes swelling, numbness, pain, and possible amputation. Trench foot was an environmental injury rather than a direct combat wound.',71)
q('Trench life','Which combination best describes the health risks emphasized in the trench slides?','Damp conditions, poor sanitation, vermin, and limited effective treatment for infection','Dry shelter, regular changes of clothing, and widespread antibiotic use|Reliable clean water but no exposure to cold or parasites|Injuries almost entirely limited to soldiers crossing no man’s land','Soldiers faced environmental and medical dangers even outside assaults. The slides emphasize that effective antibiotics were not available to them.',69,73)
q('Trench life','What can a soldier’s account of rats in a dugout most directly establish?','Evidence of that soldier’s experience and the conditions they described','The exact rat population across every front|The proportion of all soldiers killed by disease|The strategic effectiveness of a planned offensive|The opinions of all civilians about the war|The casualty total for the entire army','Testimony provides specific evidence about lived experience. It does not alone justify precise statistical or universal claims.',70)
q('Trench life','What does the comparison between recruiting images and trench photographs ask students to examine?','The gap between idealized military service and the realities of war','The difference between the Eastern Front and the Pacific theater|The reason every volunteer supported the same political party|The technical superiority of one nation’s cameras','The sequence contrasts honor, duty, and enthusiasm with death and hardship. It is an argument about representation and experience.',75,78)
q('Trench life','Which distinction is most useful when using the film material in the slides?','A dramatic film can interpret war, while archival material is evidence that still requires context.','A modern dramatic film is automatically a firsthand record of 1914.|Restored archival footage cannot contain choices about selection or narration.|A film clip proves that every soldier shared the experience it depicts.|Any visual source is less useful than a source containing words.','All Quiet on the Western Front is a dramatic representation; They Shall Not Grow Old uses archival footage. Both should be analyzed for what they can and cannot show.',67,97)
q('Trench life','Which condition described in the quotations directly concerns parasites in soldiers’ clothing?','Lice infestation','Trench foot|Shell shock|Gas poisoning|Frostbite|Shrapnel wounds|Malnutrition','The lice passage describes scraping insects off clothing and bodies. Trench foot relates to wet and cold; shell shock refers to a different range of wartime symptoms.',73)
q('Total war','How does the Eastern Front differ from the Western Front in the slides?','It generally involved more mobile fighting across central and eastern Europe.','It involved no trenches and caused relatively few losses.|It remained entirely outside the territory of the Russian Empire.|It was a naval front centered on the English Channel.','The contrast is relative: eastern fighting was more mobile, but trenches and very heavy losses still existed.',79,80)
q('Total war','Which development connected Russian wartime losses to withdrawal from the war?','Unrest and revolution were followed by the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk.','Russia received the Shandong concession and signed Versailles.|The League ordered Russia to become neutral in 1914.|Russia defeated Germany at the Somme and imposed reparations.|The United States negotiated Russian entry into the Triple Alliance.','The slides connect heavy losses to unrest, the 1917 Revolution, and the 1918 treaty ending Russia’s participation.',80)
q('Total war','Which treaty took Russia out of WWI?','Brest-Litovsk','Versailles|London|Lausanne|Trianon|Sèvres','Brest-Litovsk was signed in 1918 by the Bolshevik government and the Central Powers. Versailles was the later peace treaty with Germany.',80)
q('Total war','Which policy most directly illustrates total war on the home front?','Redirecting civilian industry and rationing goods to support military needs','Limiting all military spending to private donations|Leaving production entirely unchanged despite shortages|Separating national finances completely from the war effort','Total war draws civilian society and the economy into the conflict. The slides list economic controls, rationing, and war finance.',81)
q('Total war','What was a war bond?','A loan made by a purchaser to the government to finance the war','An agreement requiring an ally to enter the war|A limit on the amount of food a household could buy|A government order preventing publication of military news|A payment Germany made under the Versailles reparations system','Buying a bond provided government funding with a promise of repayment. It was distinct from an alliance, rationing, censorship, or postwar reparations.',81,83)
q('Total war','Which term refers to bringing industries under government ownership or control?','Nationalization','Rationing|Mobilization|Censorship|Reparations|Self-determination|Ratification|Extraterritoriality','Nationalization is an economic control. Nationalism, a different word, concerns national identity and political allegiance.',81)
q('Total war','How do censorship and propaganda differ?','Censorship restricts information; propaganda attempts to shape beliefs or behavior.','Censorship raises loans; propaganda sets ration limits.|Censorship applies only to soldiers; propaganda applies only to neutral countries.|Censorship and propaganda both mean the government owns all factories.','Both can support morale, but they work differently. A message urging enlistment is propaganda; suppressing a report is censorship.',81,84)
q('Propaganda','In the “I Want You” poster, Uncle Sam is an example of which element?','A symbol representing the United States','A statistical measure of enlistment|A military treaty obligation|A geographical label showing a front line|An official record of every soldier’s motives','A recognizable figure stands for the nation. The poster’s words add a direct appeal, but the figure itself is a symbol.',84,91)
q('Propaganda','What is the strongest main-message reading of “Daddy, what did YOU do in the Great War?”','Enlist now so that your children will view your service with pride rather than disappointment.','Purchase a bond so that the government can immediately end all rationing.|Support territorial independence for all colonial populations.|Reject military service because it separates fathers from their children.','The poster imagines a future family conversation to use anticipated guilt and pride. Its persuasive purpose should be inferred from both words and image.',87)
q('Propaganda','Which persuasive strategy is central to “Destroy This Mad Brute”?','Dehumanizing the enemy to make enlistment seem a defense against danger','Presenting a balanced comparison of military budgets|Explaining the legal terms of an armistice|Using a neutral map to describe transport routes|Reporting the results of a public-opinion survey|Promoting reconciliation through shared cultural traditions','The exaggerated threatening creature presents the enemy as monstrous. This is emotional persuasion, not a neutral description of an entire population.',90)
q('Propaganda','What does a poster urging the purchase of Victory Bonds mainly ask its audience to do?','Finance the war through lending to the government','Join a diplomatic mission to Paris|Increase household consumption of scarce food|Accept a new national border|Serve in a frontline infantry unit','The immediate requested action is financial support. Such posters demonstrate civilian participation in total war.',83,85)
q('Propaganda','Which question best identifies the “main message” of a propaganda poster?','What does the creator want the audience to believe or do?','Which year had the greatest overall military spending?|How many people accepted the message after seeing it?|Was every detail drawn at a mathematically accurate scale?','Main message concerns intended persuasion. Measuring actual influence is a separate historical question requiring more evidence.',84)
q('Propaganda','“Women of Britain Say—GO!” most directly uses which appeal?','Family expectations and gender roles to encourage enlistment','A promise that women alone would negotiate the peace|Evidence that all women held identical political opinions|An explanation of the military clauses of Versailles|An argument for abandoning imperial possessions|A neutral calculation of casualty rates|A demand that soldiers stop buying government bonds','The poster uses an image of women and children to frame enlistment as expected duty. It cannot establish that all women agreed with its message.',89)
q('Propaganda','Why is a recruiting poster insufficient evidence that most people wanted war?','It shows an attempt to persuade, not how the whole audience responded.','Recruiting posters contain no evidence about government goals.|Visual messages were never intended to influence civilians.|A poster can be used as evidence only if it includes statistics.','Intent and reception are different. The poster can reveal appeals, targets, and assumptions, while audience response requires other sources.',83,92)
q('Global war','Which evidence best supports calling WWI a global conflict?','Colonial troops and resources, fighting beyond Europe, and disruption of international trade','The fact that every battle took place within Germany|The absence of any military participation from outside Europe|The complete independence of European economies from overseas trade|The fact that every country in the world formally declared war','A world war need not involve every state. Imperial networks, overseas campaigns, and worldwide economic effects made this conflict global.',94,96)
q('Global war','Why must the “British Empire” total in the casualty chart be read carefully?','It includes forces from several imperial and dominion territories, not only Britain.','It includes only soldiers from England and excludes Scotland.|It counts all Allied losses as British losses.|It represents civilian deaths in Germany.|It excludes all troops who served overseas.','The footnote includes Canada, Australia, New Zealand, India, and South Africa alongside Britain. The category reflects the global nature of participation.',112)
q('U.S. entry','What alliance did the Zimmermann Telegram propose?','Germany and Mexico, if Germany and the United States went to war','Germany and the United States, if Mexico invaded Britain|Britain and Mexico, if Russia left the Allies|France and Japan, if Germany invaded Belgium|Germany and China, if Japan returned Shandong|Russia and Mexico, if the United States joined the League','The proposal was conditional on a German-American war. It did not establish that Mexico accepted or launched an attack.',102,103)
q('U.S. entry','Which territories did the telegram propose helping Mexico recover?','Texas, New Mexico, and Arizona','Texas, California, and Nevada|Arizona, California, and Utah|New Mexico, Colorado, and Oklahoma','The decoded message and the map both identify Texas, New Mexico, and Arizona. The offer was intended to encourage Mexico to join Germany.',102,104)
q('U.S. entry','Which country intercepted and shared the telegram with the United States?','Britain','France|Russia|Italy|Japan|China|Canada|Brazil|Australia|Mexico','British interception and decoding helped bring the proposal to U.S. attention. Germany sent the message, and Mexico was its intended partner.',103)
q('U.S. entry','What made unrestricted submarine warfare especially threatening to neutral states?','Ships could be attacked without the usual warning and search procedures, including neutral shipping.','It restricted attacks only to warships that had already fired first.|It guaranteed safe passage to all civilian cargo vessels.|It moved the fighting entirely onto land within neutral territory.|It required neutral governments to join the Central Powers.','The policy put neutral trade and lives at risk while seeking to cut Britain’s supplies. This helped turn the United States against Germany.',105)
q('U.S. entry','Which statement correctly connects the Lusitania to U.S. entry?','Its 1915 sinking fueled anger, but the United States did not enter until 1917.','Its 1917 sinking occurred after the U.S. declaration and had no earlier role in opinion.|Its sinking immediately caused Congress to declare war in May 1915.|It was sunk after the armistice as a protest against Versailles.','The dates matter: the Lusitania is part of the background to growing hostility, while renewed submarine warfare and the telegram were major pressures in 1917.',106,108)
q('U.S. entry','What can the U.S. export chart most reasonably support?','Formal neutrality coexisted with growing economic ties to Britain and France.','The United States had equal trade with both sides throughout the war.|A declaration of war had already been issued in 1912.|The United States exported no goods to Europe before 1917.|Trade figures alone reveal the personal opinions of all American voters.|Economic relationships had no connection to wartime policy.','Exports to the Allies rose while exports to Germany declined sharply. This challenges a simple picture of economic neutrality without proving that political neutrality had already ended.',109)
q('U.S. entry','When did Congress declare war on Germany?','April 6, 1917','June 28, 1914|August 4, 1914|May 7, 1915|November 11, 1918|June 28, 1919|March 3, 1918','The declaration came in April 1917, almost three years after the European war began and before the 1918 armistice.',107,108)
q('U.S. entry','Which reading of the decoded telegram avoids overstating the evidence?','It proposed an alliance and territorial support; it does not prove Mexico accepted.','It proves Mexico declared war on the United States that same day.|It proves Japan had already left the Allies to join Germany.|It was a peace treaty formally transferring U.S. territory.','The document is a proposal, including conditions and diplomatic instructions. A proposed action must be distinguished from an action that actually occurred.',102)
q('War’s end','What happened at 11 a.m. on November 11, 1918?','The armistice ceasefire took effect.','The Treaty of Versailles was signed.|The League of Nations admitted the United States.|Germany and Russia signed Brest-Litovsk.|The Paris Peace Conference opened.','The ceasefire took effect at 11 a.m.; the armistice had been signed earlier. Final peace terms were negotiated afterward.',110)
q('War’s end','Which combination best explains why Germany sought an armistice?','Military defeats, exhausted resources, worsening conditions, and internal unrest','A completed peace treaty, stable supplies, and continued military advances|U.S. withdrawal, Russian reentry, and the abolition of all naval blockades|A League order already enforced by all the major powers|The success of the original plan to defeat France within weeks|The fulfillment of Germany’s territorial aims in western Europe','The slides list interacting military, economic, and political pressures. A ceasefire resulted before a full peace agreement existed.',110)
q('War’s end','What is the most accurate relationship between shell shock and PTSD?','Shell shock was a wartime term covering symptoms that overlap with, but are not identical to, modern PTSD.','They are identical diagnostic labels with unchanged criteria since 1914.|Shell shock referred only to visible facial wounds.|PTSD was the official term used in WWI trench medical records.','The slides use the terms to connect invisible injuries across time. Historical medical categories do not map perfectly onto modern diagnoses.',111)
q('War’s end','Which conclusion follows from the mobilization figures in the statistics slide?','The Allies mobilized more troops overall than the Central Powers.','The Central Powers mobilized almost twice as many troops as the Allies.|Every Allied country mobilized more troops than Germany.|Mobilized troops and battlefield deaths are the same category.|The figures prove each side had equal industrial production.','The slide gives approximately 42 million Allied and 23 million Central Powers troops. This is a coalition total, not a country-by-country or industrial comparison.',112)
q('War’s end','Among the Allied powers shown in the slide’s death chart, which has the largest total?','Russia','France|British Empire|Italy|United States','Russia’s listed total is 1.7 million, above France and the British Empire. Germany’s total is larger, but Germany was a Central Power.',112)
q('War’s end','Which four powers account for roughly three-quarters of the deaths in that chart?','Germany, Russia, France, and Austria-Hungary','Germany, Britain, Italy, and the United States|France, Italy, the Ottoman Empire, and the United States|Russia, the Ottoman Empire, Britain, and Italy|Germany, France, Britain, and the United States|Austria-Hungary, Italy, Britain, and the Ottoman Empire','The four largest listed totals are Germany 1.8m, Russia 1.7m, France 1.3m, and Austria-Hungary 1.2m. Together they are about 6.0m of roughly 8.0m in the chart.',112)
q('Peace settlement','Which statement best distinguishes the armistice from the peace settlement?','The armistice stopped the fighting; later treaties established terms such as borders and reparations.','The armistice established every final border; Versailles only announced the ceasefire.|The armistice admitted Germany to the League; the treaty dissolved it.|The armistice was a military attack plan; the treaty began mobilization.','Stopping combat did not resolve responsibility, compensation, military limits, or territorial claims. Those were questions for the peace negotiations.',110,113)
q('Peace settlement','Which group correctly identifies the Big Four powers?','United States, Britain, France, Italy','United States, Britain, France, Russia|Britain, France, Germany, Italy|United States, France, China, Japan|Britain, Russia, Italy, Japan','Wilson, Lloyd George, Clemenceau, and Orlando represented the four leading powers in these negotiations.',113,114)
q('Peace settlement','What was a central purpose of Wilson’s League of Nations?','Preserve peace through international cooperation and the resolution of disputes','Restore the prewar Triple Alliance under German leadership|Allocate all former colonies directly to the United States|Remove the need for governments to negotiate with one another|Guarantee that every national claim would immediately become a state','The League was intended to prevent future conflict through cooperation. Its creation did not automatically resolve every competing territorial claim.',115)
q('Peace settlement','What does self-determination mean in this unit?','A people’s claim to decide its political status and government','A victor’s unrestricted right to annex territory|A government’s power to censor all wartime news|A requirement that every state adopt identical economic policies|A rule that dynastic succession overrides national claims|An automatic guarantee that ethnic and state boundaries already match','Self-determination is a principle about political choice and sovereignty. Its inconsistent application is a central issue in the peace settlement.',115,119)
q('Peace settlement','Which difference made it difficult to apply self-determination consistently?','National populations overlapped, while the victors also pursued security, territory, and imperial interests.','Every ethnic community already lived inside an uncontested independent state.|The Allies agreed to give up all overseas possessions immediately.|Only defeated European powers had any nationalist movements.','Mixed populations made boundaries difficult; imperial interests further limited the principle outside Europe. The maps and mandate discussion expose both problems.',117,119)
q('Peace settlement','What was a League mandate?','A territory administered by a designated power under a League framework','An unrestricted submarine zone imposed around a neutral country|A loan sold to civilians to pay for a military campaign|An agreement ending Russia’s participation in the war|A state’s formal approval of the Treaty of Versailles','Mandates placed former German or Ottoman territories under administration by other powers. They were not the same as immediate independence.',119)
q('Peace settlement','Which pair of powers held the main Middle Eastern mandates shown in the slides?','Britain and France','Germany and Austria-Hungary|United States and Japan|Italy and Russia|China and Brazil','Britain was associated with Palestine, Transjordan, and Iraq; France with Syria and Lebanon. These arrangements preserved major outside influence.',119)
q('Peace settlement','What do the before-and-after European maps most clearly show?','The breakup of empires and the creation or enlargement of several states','The survival of Austria-Hungary with all its prewar borders|The disappearance of every multinational state|The transfer of all former Russian territory to Germany|The unification of Europe into one state|The end of every minority population’s political grievances','Austria-Hungary disappeared and countries such as Poland and Czechoslovakia appeared on the postwar map. Redrawn borders did not eliminate all minority questions.',117,118)
q('Peace settlement','Why did Wilson’s signature not make the United States a party to the ratified Versailles settlement?','The Senate did not give the consent required for U.S. ratification.','The League refused to allow any American participation.|Britain had sole authority to ratify treaties on behalf of the United States.|Wilson signed only the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk.','Signature and ratification are separate. Senate opposition, including concerns about League obligations, prevented U.S. ratification and membership.',120)
q('Peace settlement','Which concern helped drive U.S. opposition to League membership?','Fear that international obligations could limit U.S. freedom of action and Congress’s war powers','A demand that the United States immediately acquire all German colonies|A belief that Germany had already become a member of the Triple Entente|A requirement that the American president permanently reside in Paris|A treaty clause automatically ending U.S. trade with France','Opposition included concerns about entanglement and constitutional authority, alongside domestic political conflict. It was not a rejection of every form of diplomacy.',120)
q('Peace settlement','What does the phrase “a peace built on quicksand” suggest?','Unresolved grievances and weak support could undermine the settlement’s durability.','Every major power received all of its original demands.|The armistice had already produced a universally accepted final settlement.|Territorial disputes disappeared once the League was created.|Germany’s economy was unaffected by the settlement.|The treaty guaranteed another war on a fixed timetable.','The metaphor highlights instability and dissatisfaction. It is not proof that a later war was inevitable or that the settlement had no achievements.',121)
q('Peace settlement','Which change explains the slide’s contrast between “Shantung: 1919 v. 1922”?','German rights went to Japan in 1919; a 1922 settlement restored Chinese sovereignty in Shandong.','China gained Shandong in 1919, then transferred it to Germany in 1922.|Shandong became a British mandate in 1919, then a French colony in 1922.|Japan lost Shandong to Russia in both settlements.','Shantung is an older spelling of Shandong. The later agreement revised an outcome that had provoked major Chinese opposition in 1919.',121)
